  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/bf1c37a92b184cb3e6c81bbd2d653008f5eff0e3b2131f2f297a336881661c22.png)\n\nUsing the visual schematic above for the Cartesian product ${A}^{2}$ for $A={p,q,r},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(q,q)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(p,r)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- ${q}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "The set ${A}^{2}$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A\\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times A,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set $(A)$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set $(A)$ at each intersection.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/919ad6e16dcd1d8f02e8c15c7bd1dfa15f67232c99706dfbc1239e1f9a0f99e5.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(q,q)\\in {A}^{2}\\,\u2713$\n\n- $(p,r)\\in {A}^{2}\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${q}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
